trocardiograms in healthy North American children in the digital age. Circ Arrhythm Electrophysiol 2018;11:e005808. Pediatric Heart Network."},"comparison":{"qrs_shorter_pct":17,"r_v6_lower_pct":38,"s_v1_lower_pct":16},"notes":[{"text":"Shaded bands come from one hospital\u2019s data (ZZU, China). Below 3 years the heart-rate band follows Rijnbeek 2001 instead, because that hospital labels a toddler\u2019s normal fast heart rate as sinus tachycardia (from about 100 bpm at every age), which removes many healthy toddlers from its \u201cnormal\u201d group.","refs":["tan2025","hv1","rijnbeek2001"]},{"text":"On average across age groups, ZZU QRS durations are 17% shorter than the published medians, and its chest-lead waves are smaller (R in V6 38% lower, S in V1 16% shallower). This most likely reflects the ECG machine, the measuring method and the population, so compare the shapes of the curves more than exact numbers.","refs":["hv1","rijnbeek2001","saarel2018"]},{"text":"Only 34 of the 799 ZZU children are aged 1\u20132 years, so the ZZU curves are least certain there.","refs":["hv1"]},{"text":"Saarel 2018 S-wave values are signed voltages in the paper; they are shown here as depths (positive), like the other sources.","refs":["saarel2018"]},{"text":"The data compare different children at different ages; they do not follow children as they grow.","refs":["hv1","rijnbeek2001","saarel2018"]}]}